    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d134">

                <pb facs="0271" n="177" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg177"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">814.001 Orellana, Manuel/1: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McCafferty</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-12-17T16:00:00-07:00">December 17, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:27 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>109. Congress met today in special session and accepted the resignation of
                    Provisional President Palma and appointed General Orellana, <pb facs="0272"
                        n="178" xml:id="pg178"/> the head of the revolutionary movement, as
                    Provisional President. This appointment is illegal because it is contrary to
                    article 65 of the Constitution and the pacts of 1923.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d134-f1"/> Several Deputies have told me that the Congress was
                    surrounded by armed forces and the Deputies were forced by threats to vote for
                    General Orellana.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">McCafferty</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
